entry.summary:
In 2026, almost every Laravel project integrates 3–10 external APIs: OpenAI, Stripe, Telegram, AWS S3, Resend, Brevo, and so on. Yet most key leaks happen not because of sophisticated attacks, but due to silly mistakes: committing to git, logging them, sending them to the frontend, or calling env() in production after config:cache. Today we’ll walk through a battle-tested path — from “it just works” to “I sleep peacefully”. Never hard-code keys in your source: // ❌ Bad $openai = new OpenAI('sk-123...');
// ✅ Good # .env OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-... STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_live_...
// config/services.php return [ 'openai' => [ 'api_key' => env('OPENAI_API_KEY'), 'organization' => env('OPENAI_ORG'), ],
'stripe' => [ 'key' => env('STRIPE_KEY'), 'secre…
